2025-05-01 03:28:50 - Thread-1441 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and why they matter  
• orders.store_id : groups every order to the store that sold it.  
• stores.store_id / stores.store_name : lets us attach the readable store name to each order.  
• orders.delivery_order_id : the bridge between an order and the deliveries table (deliveries.delivery_order_id).  
• deliveries.delivery_status : tells us whether the order was in the logistics table and, if so, whether it ended as 'DELIVERED'.  

We will explore the data step‑by‑step, building toward the final result.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at the first 20 orders just to confirm basic columns
SELECT * 
FROM "orders"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. List every distinct store_id present in the orders table
SELECT DISTINCT "store_id"
FROM "orders"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Count number of orders per store (top 20)
SELECT "store_id",
       COUNT(*) AS order_qty
FROM "orders"
GROUP BY "store_id"
ORDER BY order_qty DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Attach the store name to those counts (top 20 by order volume)
SELECT o."store_id",
       s."store_name",
       COUNT(*) AS order_qty
FROM   "orders"  AS o
JOIN   "stores"  AS s
  ON   s."store_id" = o."store_id"
GROUP  BY o."store_id", s."store_name"
ORDER  BY order_qty DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Focus on the single store that has the MAX orders
--    (returns exactly one row)
SELECT o."store_id",
       s."store_name",
       COUNT(*) AS total_orders
FROM   "orders" AS o
JOIN   "stores" AS s
  ON   s."store_id" = o."store_id"
GROUP  BY o."store_id", s."store_name"
ORDER  BY total_orders DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 6. Show 20 sample orders for that top store (replace :top_store_id if needed)
SELECT *
FROM   "orders"
WHERE  "store_id" = :top_store_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Among the top store’s orders, see which ones appear in deliveries and with what status
SELECT o."order_id",
       d."delivery_status"
FROM   "orders"     AS o
LEFT   JOIN "deliveries" AS d
  ON   d."delivery_order_id" = o."delivery_order_id"
WHERE  o."store_id" = :top_store_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Aggregate for that store: total orders vs. orders that exist in deliveries table
SELECT COUNT(*)                             AS total_orders,
       COUNT(d."delivery_order_id")         AS orders_in_deliveries
FROM   "orders" AS o
LEFT   JOIN "deliveries" AS d
  ON   d."delivery_order_id" = o."delivery_order_id"
WHERE  o."store_id" = :top_store_id;
```

```sql
-- 9. Still for the top store: how many were 'DELIVERED'?
SELECT COUNT(*) AS delivered_orders
FROM   "orders" AS o
JOIN   "deliveries" AS d
  ON   d."delivery_order_id" = o."delivery_order_id"
WHERE  o."store_id" = :top_store_id
  AND  d."delivery_status" = 'DELIVERED';
```

```sql
-- 10. Final single‑shot query:
--     1) find store with most orders,
--     2) compute ratio of delivered deliveries to all its orders
SELECT t."store_id",
       s."store_name",
       t.total_orders,
       COALESCE(d.delivered_orders, 0)                        AS delivered_orders,
       COALESCE(d.delivered_orders, 0) * 1.0 / t.total_orders AS delivered_ratio
FROM  ( SELECT o."store_id",
               COUNT(*) AS total_orders
        FROM   "orders" AS o
        GROUP  BY o."store_id"
        ORDER  BY total_orders DESC
        LIMIT 1
      ) AS t
LEFT JOIN ( SELECT o."store_id",
                   COUNT(*) AS delivered_orders
            FROM   "orders" AS o
            JOIN   "deliveries" AS d
              ON   d."delivery_order_id" = o."delivery_order_id"
            WHERE  d."delivery_status" = 'DELIVERED'
            GROUP  BY o."store_id"
          ) AS d
  ON d."store_id" = t."store_id"
JOIN  "stores"  AS s
  ON s."store_id" = t."store_id";
```

The final query (10) directly returns:

• store_id and store_name of the store with the highest order count  
• total_orders for that store  
• delivered_orders that achieved 'DELIVERED' status  
• delivered_ratio = delivered_orders ÷ total_orders  

Replace :top_store_id in queries 6–9 with the value shown by query 5.
